WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.500
Socket-safe C begins with the assumption that every socket is a trust boundary.

2
00:00:04.600 --> 00:00:08.600
The peer may be expected, but the data is still external.

3
00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:13.020
The endpoint may be local, but local does not automatically mean authorized.

4
00:00:13.120 --> 00:00:17.920
The service may be internal, but internal systems still need clear boundaries.

5
00:00:18.200 --> 00:00:22.760
Restrict endpoint exposure and run services with least privilege.

6
00:00:22.860 --> 00:00:35.340
Binding choices, interface selection, service accounts, firewall policy, container networking, and deployment configuration all shape who can reach the service and what the process can affect if something goes wrong.

7
00:00:35.620 --> 00:00:43.140
Validate protocol messages before allocation, copying, deep parsing, command dispatch, or state changes.

8
00:00:43.240 --> 00:00:45.320
TCP needs explicit framing.

9
00:00:45.420 --> 00:00:49.020
UDP needs size, source, and content checks.

10
00:00:49.120 --> 00:00:55.520
Lengths, commands, flags, and payloads need consistent limits and schema expectations.

11
00:00:55.800 --> 00:01:01.720
Handle partial I/O, timeouts, disconnects, and cleanup as normal operating conditions.

12
00:01:01.820 --> 00:01:08.780
Socket code should expect incomplete reads, partial writes, slow peers, dropped connections, and parser failures.

13
00:01:08.880 --> 00:01:13.920
Resource release needs to work on every path, not only during graceful shutdown.

14
00:01:14.200 --> 00:01:19.800
Finally, protect sensitive data with appropriate transport security and logging discipline.

15
00:01:19.900 --> 00:01:29.180
Use tests, static analysis, sanitizers, runtime diagnostics, and code review to keep behavior predictable under failure and load.

16
00:01:29.280 --> 00:01:34.160
Secure socket programming is disciplined boundary management applied to every connection.
